  "Summon, copy, gone. Everything else — setup, settings, sync — is there when you go looking for it and invisible when you are not.": "",
  "The 34-byte header is self-describing and bound as associated data, so a tampered version, KDF id, salt or nonce fails the tag check before anything is decrypted.": "",
  "The browser extension and its WASM boundary": "",
  "The configured modifier plus a digit — ⌘1 to ⌘5, Ctrl elsewhere — copies that row's code. Each row shows its own badge, so you never count positions.": "",
  "The derived key lives in chrome.storage.session, never the passphrase, and a chrome.alarms timer clears it after the interval you set in Options.": "",
  "The desktop app serves the browser extension over loopback on port 4849. Pairing codes expire, each browser gets its own bearer token, and you can revoke any of them.": "",
  "The desktop shell, keyring handling and IPC surface": "",
  "The extension runs the same Rust core compiled to WebAssembly. It recognises the 2FA field, offers the matching account, and fills the code — without a login or a copy-paste round trip.": "",
  "The frontend measures itself and the window follows, so short lists do not leave empty space.": "",
  "The install prompt is short on purpose. There is no \"read your data on all websites\" line, because the extension never asks for one.": "",
  "The interface itself": "",
  "The issuer's copy": "",
  "The kdf_id byte is versioned, so Argon2id can be added later without invalidating a single existing vault.": "",
  "The key exists only while you're unlocked.": "",
  "The key lives in memory behind a lock and is zeroized on drop. An idle watchdog locks the vault after a timeout you set.": "",
  "The localhost sync bridge and merge semantics": "",
  "The manifest is the commit point. If a second browser wrote while you did, the Rust merge runs — newest-wins with tombstones — instead of one of you losing an account.": "",
  "The popup, on a key": "",
  "The same Rust core, compiled to WebAssembly and running in the tab you're already in. Chrome, Edge and Firefox are built from one manifest, so a feature never lands on one and quietly skips the other.": "",
  "The same vault inside Chrome and Firefox. Detects the 2FA field and fills the code.": "",
  "The sealed vault is base64-chunked across chrome.storage.sync at 6 KB a chunk. Read the raw storage and you get a manifest and opaque blobs — nothing legible.": "",
  "The vault format, KDF and sealing in twofau-core": "",
  "The vault is a single encrypted blob on your disk. Codes are computed in Rust and handed to the UI already formatted — the interface never sees a shared secret, and neither does a server, because there isn't one.": "",
  "The whole vault — accounts, tombstones and revision metadata — is a single JSON document, sealed as one AES-256-GCM ciphertext and rewritten on every mutation.": "",
  "THE WINDOW": "",
  "The window starts hidden and is anchored to the tray icon each time it opens — below the menu bar on macOS, above the taskbar on Windows and Linux, and clamped so it never lands off-screen on a second monitor.": "",
  "There is no 2FAu server and no account, so there is nothing to breach on our side. The extension's vault rides your browser's own sync, sealed before it leaves the device.": "",
  "There is no Secure Enclave equivalent across four platforms, so the vault is passphrase-derived. The key is never written to disk — only the salt it was derived from.": "",
  "this site": "",
  "THREAT MODEL": "",
  "THREE PLATFORMS": "",
  "Time drift check": "",
  "Time-based codes rotate on their own; counter-based ones advance when you ask, so hardware-style tokens work the same as everything else.": "",
  "TOTP and HOTP": "",
  "TOTP breaks when the machine clock drifts. 2FAu samples a trusted time over HTTPS four times, keeps the fastest round-trip, and stores the offset — not the instant.": "",
  "TOTP is a shared secret by design. Whoever issued the seed can still generate the same codes you can.": "",
